---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
/**
 * SPEC §14.10 — "state this in a short privacy note linked in the footer".
 *
 * ## This page is a promise, so it changed when the behaviour did
 *
 * It used to import `RETENTION_MONTHS` from the pruning job, so the stated
 * window could not drift from the window actually enforced. There is no
 * database and no pruning job now: everything is emailed and not stored, which
 * makes the retention section a statement about an inbox rather than a table.
 *
 * The entry gate added a second thing to declare, and this page is the only
 * place a visitor can find out what it takes. The list of browser facts here
 * must match `VisitorSchema` in `src/lib/visitor.ts` exactly — under-stating it
 * is the failure that matters, and over-stating it is not much better.
 *
 * ## Voice
 *
 * BRAND §8: plain, specific, slightly dry. No "we value your privacy", no
 * cookie-banner boilerplate for cookies the site does not set. It says what is
 * collected, what is done to it, how long it stays, and how to have it removed.
 * Anything longer would be padding, and padding is what makes these unread.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — mh.neri.ph"
  description="What the contact form collects, how long it is kept, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <article class="flex max-w-[68ch] flex-col gap-lg py-xl sm:py-2xl">
    <h1 class="font-display text-headline-lg">Privacy</h1>

    <p>
      No advertising, no third-party embeds, and no cookies. What this site does
      collect, it collects because a form asked you for it — set out below in
      full.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">
      What the entry form collects, and why
    </h2>
    <p>
      You are asked for an email address before the portfolio opens, and
      optionally a name. That is used for one thing: to tell me someone read it.
      There is no mailing list, and nothing is sent to you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Alongside it, the page reports what your browser volunteers about the
      visit: <strong
        >which page you landed on, the referring URL, your user-agent string,
        browser language, time zone, screen size and window size</strong
      >, plus the time. That is the whole list. There is no analytics script, no
      tracking pixel, no fingerprinting, and nothing that follows you to another
      site.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your answer is remembered in your browser's local storage so you are not
      asked again. It stays on your device, is not a cookie, is not sent
      anywhere, and clearing your site data removes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Downloading the resume reports the same set</strong>, so I know
      who took a copy. If you got past the entry form, that download is linked
      to the email you gave.
    </p>
    <p>
      Being plain about the obvious: the entry form is a courtesy, not a lock.
      The pages behind it are ordinary public HTML, and anyone determined to
      read them without answering can.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">
      What the contact form collects
    </h2>
    <p>
      Your name, your email address, and your message. Nothing else is asked
      for, and nothing is inferred.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing is derived alongside it: a salted SHA-256 hash of your IP
      address — <strong>never the address itself</strong> — which is what the rate
      limit counts against. It is held in memory for the length of the rate-limit
      window and never written to disk. The hash cannot be reversed to an address,
      and no readable form of it exists at any point.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">Where it goes</h2>
    <p>
      All of it — messages, entry-form details, download notices — is emailed to
      one inbox, mine. This site has no database, so no copy is kept here: once
      the email is sent, nothing about you remains on the server. It is not
      shared, sold, or passed to anyone else. Server logs record that a
      submission happened; they do not record the message, your name, your email
      address, or your IP.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">How long it is kept</h2>
    <p>
      For as long as the email sits in my inbox, which is a mailbox I read
      rather than a system with a retention policy. There is no automated
      deletion, and claiming otherwise would be inventing a schedule nothing
      enforces.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">Having it deleted</h2>
    <p>
      Ask, and it goes. Send the request through the <a
        class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-deep-teal focus-visible:text-deep-teal"
        href="/#contact">contact form</a
      > — say you want your earlier message removed and roughly when you sent it.
      No account, no form to fill in, no waiting period.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
